            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was observed along the basebaords where the floor connects with the crawlspace. 

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. As you mentioned moisture typically accumulates along the windows, I recommend also installing a humidistat to the new bathroom fan. This will allow for a more "automated" humidity control, by turning on and off only as required based on the applied setting. 

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 

I suspect the majority of the air leakage is coming from the crawlspace, as the majority of the floor above the crawlspace is not air sealed from the home. This allows for easy pathways of air infiltration to enter from the cold crawlspace (as we noticed around the stairs by the laundry room). I recommend to air seal the crawlspace floor using polyethylene plastic. When doing so, please be sure to insulate underneath the small stairs leading to the laundry room. 



Savings: For a minimum 20% improvement you could potentially save $ 460 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.5 GJ.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 810 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.7 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house appeared to have 7.0 inches (R28) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3.5 inches (R12) of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R40.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R40, you could potentially save $ 150 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ. </ceilings>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all 12 windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3.  In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the 12 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 990 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.0 GJ.  

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 170 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 1,000 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

FURNACE UPGRADE

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower and an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,750 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 41.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOODSTOVE

Savings (with current oil furnace):

By using your current wood stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 2,400 per year in energy savings. This assumes a cost of $ 100 per cord of wood for harvesting your own. </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COMBUSTION AIR

I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnace. 

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace is satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace is operating.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke detectors, however they appeared to be older units. I did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

